  "query_preview": "..\n(Friday night through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1207 PM CDT Thu Sep 24 2026\n\nA broad upper shortwave trough will keep rain chances increased \nacross the FA late Friday night into Saturday morning before upper \nridging takes hold of the Central and Southern Plains by Sunday, \nhelping squash rain chances. While confidence is high in a pattern \nchance from the upper ridge by mid week, models are split on the \nactual pattern. The ECMWF develops a H566 upper low over the Four \nCorners by Tuesday evening before moving over the Texas and Oklahoma \nPanhandles by early Thursday. The GFS keeps the upper trough as an \nopen wave. Both scenarios make use of the rich tropical moisture \nfrom Hurricane Polo in the eastern Pacific and bring very good, \nwidespread rain chances to the region. The ECMWF solution would \nbring the best and heaviest rain chances. Both solutions also bring \na cold front into the FA between late Tuesday and early Wednesday, \nthe GFS having the stronger and more aggressive front. This would \nalso enhance lift and keep rain chances decently high (as far as \nlong term forecasts go). This also falls in line with historical El \nNino trends. October, historically, tends to be a fairly wet month \nfor the South Plains region. Though the forecast is still during \nSeptember, it is just days from October.",
